      "Language": "chi",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To explore the relevance between carotid plaque and the severity of coronary artery disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A total of 207 patients(149 males,58 females) undergoing coronary angiography were definitely diagnosed with coronary heart disease. They were divided into 3 groups of one-vessel disease (1VD, n = 69), two-vessel disease (2VD, n = 57) and three-or-more vessel disease (3VD, n = 81) by the number of involved vessels. Their physical findings, biochemical data and carotid ultrasonographic results were collected. And the scores of Crouse, Gensini and Leaman were calculated to compare the relevance."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "No significant differences existed among all three groups except for a higher age and the presence of plaque in 2VD and 3VD groups than 1VD group ((63 ± 11) and (60 ± 11) vs (57 ± 10)years old, 82%(n = 47) and 81%(n = 66) vs 65%(n = 45), both P < 0.05) . Crouse had a positive correlation with Gensini (r = 0.455, P < 0.01) and Leaman scores (r = 0.358, P < 0.01) ."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Assessment of plaque scores by carotid ultrasonography is clinically relevant for predicting the severity of coronary artery disease."
        }
      ]},
